<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kamiros</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 408 BCE (approx.) · capital Kamiros</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Farming city on the west coast of Rhodes, one of the island's three ancient cities, whose terraced streets survive in remarkable condition.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kamiros was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorian Greeks</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> on a slope above the west coast of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> across the sea. It was one of the three Rhodian cities, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lindos">Lindos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ialysos">Ialysos</a>, and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorian-hexapolis">Dorian Hexapolis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Unlike <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lindos">Lindos</a>, Kamiros relied mainly on farming of grain, figs, olives and wine. Its cemeteries have yielded rich finds of Archaic East Greek pottery and jewellery. The city fell under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and then belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>. Its acropolis held a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> Kameiras.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 408 BCE Kamiros joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lindos">Lindos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ialysos">Ialysos</a> to found the city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and lost its political independence. It continued as a town; after earthquakes it was rebuilt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, and its houses and cistern can still be seen.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 900 BCE – 408 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66